# Review

The title and opening claim apply to selection-based learning over any interdependent store of instructions, hypotheses, checks and scripts. Two domains of narrower vocabulary appear in the body, and both are framed.

Software-debugging terms such as stack trace, imports, `git bisect`, unit test and diffs are deliberately chosen points of comparison. The note introduces them as an analogy ("large software codebases have no chain rule either") and then states where the analogy fails. They are therefore not residue.

The chain of "routing instruction", "skill", "retained convention" and "validator" resembles Commonplace's own architecture. The note offers it after a colon as an illustration of how artifacts become interdependent, not as a requirement of the claim.

LLM-specific terms such as "context saturation" and "prompt interference" fit the note's domain. The softer-traces bullet places the argument in LLM-loaded natural-language instructions. No unframed narrower-domain framing overcommits the note.
